1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,777
Welcome to SocialConnect, a complete Social Media Database Management System.

2
00:00:05,777 --> 00:00:11,554
Built with Python Flask, PostgreSQL, and modern JavaScript, this project

3
00:00:11,554 --> 00:00:17,332
demonstrates how a real-world web interface interacts with a relational

4
00:00:17,332 --> 00:00:23,109
database across the entire stack. Let's explore how it works.

5
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:28,245
Modern social platforms require robust databases to store posts, record

6
00:00:28,245 --> 00:00:33,381
likes, and deliver private messages safely. SocialConnect addresses this by

7
00:00:33,381 --> 00:00:38,518
connecting a responsive web frontend to PostgreSQL, ensuring referential integrity,

8
00:00:38,518 --> 00:00:43,654
reliable data persistence, and organized relational storage for all community

9
00:00:43,654 --> 00:00:48,790
interactions.

10
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:54,592
SocialConnect provides rich core features: publishing posts with optional images,

11
00:00:54,592 --> 00:01:00,395
browsing a live chronological feed, keyword search, like and unlike

12
00:01:00,395 --> 00:01:06,197
tracking, threaded comments, user profile inspection, and real-time direct messaging

13
00:01:06,197 --> 00:01:12,000
between community members, alongside a dark and light theme toggle.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,677
The system follows a three-tier architecture. The frontend uses HTML,

15
00:01:17,677 --> 00:01:23,355
CSS, and modern JavaScript. The backend is powered by Python

16
00:01:23,355 --> 00:01:29,033
Flask with psycopg2 for database connectivity. At the foundation is

17
00:01:29,033 --> 00:01:34,711
PostgreSQL, storing relational data. Communication between tiers uses standard HTTP

18
00:01:34,711 --> 00:01:40,389
requests and structured JSON responses.

19
00:01:40,389 --> 00:01:45,333
Here is the application running live in the browser. Users

20
00:01:45,333 --> 00:01:50,276
can select an author from the dropdown, write their message,

21
00:01:50,276 --> 00:01:55,219
and publish it instantly. The feed updates dynamically without a

22
00:01:55,219 --> 00:02:00,163
page refresh, displaying author names, timestamps, and like counts. Users

23
00:02:00,163 --> 00:02:05,106
can search posts using case-insensitive matching, view complete profiles, or

24
00:02:05,106 --> 00:02:10,049
open the messaging page for direct conversations.

25
00:02:10,049 --> 00:02:15,727
When a user performs an action, JavaScript captures the event

26
00:02:15,727 --> 00:02:21,405
and sends an asynchronous HTTP request with a JSON payload

27
00:02:21,405 --> 00:02:27,083
to Flask. Flask validates the request, executes parameterized SQL queries

28
00:02:27,083 --> 00:02:32,761
through psycopg2 against the PostgreSQL database, and returns a JSON

29
00:02:32,761 --> 00:02:38,439
response. JavaScript then updates the user interface seamlessly.

30
00:02:38,439 --> 00:02:44,347
The backend exposes eleven RESTful API endpoints. Key routes include

31
00:02:44,347 --> 00:02:50,255
GET /api/posts for retrieving the feed, POST /api/posts for inserting

32
00:02:50,255 --> 00:02:56,163
new posts, and /api/posts/like which implements conflict handling. All database

33
00:02:56,163 --> 00:03:02,071
queries use parameterization to eliminate SQL injection vulnerabilities, with strict

34
00:03:02,071 --> 00:03:07,979
commit and rollback transaction management.

35
00:03:07,979 --> 00:03:14,319
The PostgreSQL database, named social_media_db, contains five relational tables: users,

36
00:03:14,319 --> 00:03:20,659
posts, likes, comments, and messages. Every table defines a primary

37
00:03:20,659 --> 00:03:26,999
key. Foreign keys connect posts, comments, and likes to users,

38
00:03:26,999 --> 00:03:33,339
enforcing referential integrity. A composite unique constraint on likes prevents

39
00:03:33,339 --> 00:03:39,679
duplicate entries at the database level.

40
00:03:39,679 --> 00:03:45,607
SocialConnect highlights fundamental DBMS concepts in practice: primary and foreign

41
00:03:45,607 --> 00:03:51,534
key constraints, multi-table joins, aggregate functions like COUNT, insert operations

42
00:03:51,534 --> 00:03:57,462
with the RETURNING clause, upsert logic using ON CONFLICT DO

43
00:03:57,462 --> 00:04:03,389
NOTHING, and transaction safety using explicit commit and rollback calls.

44
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:07,458
In this query demonstration, we see the real SQL statements

45
00:04:07,458 --> 00:04:11,526
executed by the system. The main feed query performs an

46
00:04:11,526 --> 00:04:15,594
INNER JOIN between posts and users, combined with a LEFT

47
00:04:15,594 --> 00:04:19,663
JOIN on the likes table. It aggregates like counts using

48
00:04:19,663 --> 00:04:23,731
COUNT DISTINCT, groups results by post ID, and sorts them

49
00:04:23,731 --> 00:04:27,799
newest first.

50
00:04:27,799 --> 00:04:32,656
Let's trace a complete end-to-end user action. When a post

51
00:04:32,656 --> 00:04:37,513
is published, the browser sends a POST request with JSON

52
00:04:37,513 --> 00:04:42,369
data. Flask verifies the fields, opens a database connection, and

53
00:04:42,369 --> 00:04:47,226
executes an INSERT with RETURNING. PostgreSQL saves the record, generates

54
00:04:47,226 --> 00:04:52,083
the post ID, and returns it. The interface displays the

55
00:04:52,083 --> 00:04:56,939
new post instantly with zero likes.

56
00:04:56,939 --> 00:05:02,370
In conclusion, SocialConnect demonstrates full-stack integration, robust API design, and

57
00:05:02,370 --> 00:05:07,800
practical relational database management. Future enhancements could introduce user authentication,

58
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:13,230
image storage, and pagination. Thank you for watching this presentation

59
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:18,660
of SocialConnect.
